    <title>Irish School Maths</title>
    <subtitle>Making Maths as Simple as Possible</subtitle>
    <frontmatter>
      <bibinfo>
        <author>
          <personname>Ronan Downes</personname>
        </author>
      </bibinfo>
      <titlepage>
        <titlepage-items/>
      </titlepage>

      <preface xml:id="welcome">
        <title>Welcome</title>
        <p>Welcome to <em>Irish School Maths</em>, a free, developing mathematics book for students and teachers. It lives online, can be printed, and brings together explanations, worked examples, exercises, investigations and interactive resources.</p>
        <p>My aim is to make mathematics as simple as possible without making it superficial. Clear procedures matter, but so do ideas, reasoning, judgement and the freedom to choose between methods. I hope this book helps students grow in understanding, confidence and independence.</p>
        <p>You need not read the book from beginning to end. Material is organised by course and mathematical strand, not by a prescribed year-by-year teaching sequence. Links can lead back to earlier explanations or forward to greater challenge. Teachers can share a particular worked example, exercise or investigation directly; students can find the help they need and return to the task in hand.</p>
        <p>Graphs, diagrams, tables, algebra and interactive applications offer different ways to explore the same idea. The aim is to make support available when it is useful, without creating dependence on it.</p>
        <p>This book is dedicated to Airida, Gabija and Erik. I also thank the many students I have taught over the years, especially those who have demanded clearer explanations, more careful pacing and better tools that allow them to take greater control of their learning. Their questions have helped shape this book.</p>
        <p>The <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/maths/" visual="Maths Apps"/>, <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/calculator/" visual="calculators"/> and <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/pastpapers/" visual="past papers"/> complement the book. A further collection of <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/calculator/resources/" visual="free textbooks and mathematics resources"/> is available separately.</p>
      </preface>

      <preface xml:id="project-preface">
        <title>Preface</title>
        <p>Mathematics is not simply a collection of procedures to be memorised. Students need opportunities to understand principles, recognise connections, communicate their thinking and choose appropriate methods. Clear explanations and deliberate practice are important, but they should develop alongside exploration, discussion and problem-solving.</p>
        <p>I call one common misconception <em>the procedural fluency trap</em>: the assumption that students must master procedures before developing conceptual understanding, strategic competence, adaptive reasoning or habits of problem-solving. Procedural fluency is essential, but it is not a prerequisite for all other aspects of mathematical proficiency. Problem-solving is not just something students do after learning mathematics; it is one of the ways they learn it.</p>
        <p>When students encounter more than one sound method, they can compare approaches and exercise mathematical judgement. The worked examples, investigations, exercises and interactive tools in this book are intended to encourage that independence, while providing accessible routes back to foundational knowledge when needed.</p>
        <p>Transition Year should offer something different from simply repeating Junior Cycle or teaching Leaving Certificate topics in advance. The <url href="https://www.curriculumonline.ie/senior-cycle/transition-year/" visual="2024 Transition Year Programme Statement"/> emphasises flexible, student-centred learning across personal growth, being a learner, civic and community engagement, and career exploration. In that spirit, the TY mathematics material will focus on investigations, modelling, real-world questions, collaboration and student choice.</p>
        <p>The book is published using <em>PreTeXt</em>, which supports structured digital reading, accessible mathematical content and print formats. It is a work in progress and will continue to develop as the materials are used and improved.</p>
      </preface>

      <preface xml:id="for-students">
        <title>For Students</title>
        <p>Start with the mathematics you are studying. You do not have to begin at page one. If an earlier idea needs revisiting, follow a link or use the contents to find it, then return to your current work.</p>
        <p>Try to do more than copy a worked solution. Predict, attempt, test and explain. Use diagrams, examples, interactive apps and exercises to discover what changes, what stays the same and why a method works.</p>
        <p>Use learning intentions to see what you are aiming for, and answers to check your reasoning rather than replace it. The goal is to become steadily more capable of working things out for yourself.</p>
      </preface>

      <preface xml:id="about-the-author">
        <title>About the Author</title>
        <p>Ronan Downes is a Mathematics teacher based in Limerick, Ireland. He studied Physics at NUI Galway and later graduated with an honours degree in Physical Optoelectronics from the University of Essex. After completing a Higher Diploma in Education, he taught Physics, Mathematics and Science in secondary schools for four years.</p>
        <p>He then moved into telecommunications, working as a systems engineer and access engineer with Esat Digifone. He later became self-employed in multimedia, including photography, video production and web development.</p>
        <p>A lifelong interest in learning brought him back to university, where he completed a <url href="https://www.ul.ie/study/postgraduate/mathematical-modelling-msc" visual="Master's degree in Mathematical Modelling"/> at the University of Limerick. He subsequently returned to classroom teaching and later completed a <url href="https://www.cct.ie/course/masters-data-analytics-postgraduate-msc/" visual="Master's degree in Data Analytics"/> at CCT College Dublin. His experience in modelling, programming and computational thinking informs the explanations and interactive resources in this book.</p>
        <p>He has also worked for many years as an examiner with the State Examinations Commission. That experience has reinforced the importance of teaching students to communicate their mathematical thinking.</p>
        <p>His aim is to reduce unnecessary cognitive load without removing the ideas, reasoning and choices that give mathematics its meaning. His guiding principle is inspired by a saying commonly attributed to Albert Einstein: <em>Making maths as simple as possible, but not too simple.</em></p>
      </preface>
    </frontmatter>

